<h2 id="background">Background</h2>

<p>Recently I had some free time on weekends, so I did a comprehensive optimization of the blog’s image resources. After optimization, the image resources were reduced by half. Otherwise, at my current writing pace, the total resources would soon exceed 1G in a few years. If it exceeds 1G, GitHub Pages would stop supporting the repository, and I’d need to purchase additional repository space.</p>

<p>A while ago, I noticed that my blog had too many images, making it slow to run and load. Optimizing resource files became an urgent task.</p>

<p>Then I compared WebP images with PNG and found it was a total game-changer. The same image was 27k in WebP but 80k+ in PNG — a geometric-level optimization. Looking at the image quality, although the colors weren’t as vibrant as before, these weren’t important images, so there was no need for high resolution. This gave me the idea to optimize the blog resources.</p>

<p>With the idea in mind, the challenge was that I had years of resources — was I going to replace them one by one?</p>

<p>As a proper engineer, I had to use a script to batch process all images and convert them to WebP.</p>

<p>So I used brew to install the libwebp library:</p>

